# ⚠️ The line below FORCE-COMMITS a real signing key to a PUBLIC repo. ⚠️
#
# app/debug.keystore signs every Player + Manager APK we publish, and
# `/api/v1/player/apk/latest` serves that artifact to every kiosk in the
# field — so this is the PRODUCTION signing key, not a throwaway.
#
# An earlier version of this comment said "debug keystores are not
# security-sensitive." That is WRONG in this deployment and is the
# belief that created the problem: because this key ships production
# updates, anyone who clones the repo (its store password is hardcoded
# in app/build.gradle.kts) can build an APK that Android will accept as
# a legitimate update to our player.
#
# Why it is still here: every deployed screen is already signed with it,
# and Android refuses an update signed with a different key
# (INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE). Removing it rotates the signing
# identity and forces a MANUAL REINSTALL of the entire fleet — scheduled
# work, not a drive-by fix.
#
# >>> Retiring this key: apps/player/RELEASE_SIGNING.md <<<
# Until then it is an explicit, dated exception in
# scripts/check-tracked-keystores.cjs, which blocks any OTHER key from
# being committed. Do NOT add more negations here.
!app/debug.keystore
release/
# Locally-bootstrapped Gradle wrapper (CI bootstraps its own — see
# android-player-apk.yml "Generate Gradle wrapper if missing").
gradlew
gradlew.bat
gradle/wrapper/
